hi kevin hi martin  i heard you work for a car sharing  service called green  mobility and the company is using  serverless  yeah uh our dell team is really  productive thanks to serverless  computing  oh maybe you could share some tips on  how to use serverless to get your  developers more productive i would be  happy to  so kevin green mobility is a car sharing  service  yes we've run a car sharing platform in  seven european cities  we have hundreds of the cars and people  use them  for thousands of trips every day and we  have about 100  employees with headquarters in  copenhagen and we are listed on the  nasdaq main market  oh cool i guess many people want to use  cars but not necessarily own cars these  days  yeah like more and more people want to  live in cities and they're interested in  sustainability and they also prefer  sharing things rather than owning them  ah right so what kind of data processing  does the company need  to make this car sharing happen we  process hundreds of thousands of events  every day  trips starts come moves a car  trip ins and we could have built this  infrastructure for handling these events  with virtual machines  but there would have been a lot of work  instead we went with serverless what  makes it  much easier oh cool i love  serverless uh where do you store these  events kevin  we saw them in firestore we also have a  cloud segal database for other data but  we got better  data writing performance at a lower cost  with firestore  and firestore is serverless which means  less operation work for us  yeah so we will have sql and no sql  databases  ah got it machine learning is hot right  now  are you doing that too we're working on  it  we like to predict what cars will be  needed so we can put cars in those  places ahead of time  our plan is to export the firestore  database to bigger every night  then we could run reports in bigquery  and run bigquery ml  to to do the machine learning on the  data  are there any other factors that made  you go with serverless  we also have uneven demand more people  are driving  during the day than at night so it would  be inefficient to scale all computing  and databases up and down  ourselves but cloud run and firestore  don't do it automatically  ah why did you pick cloud run  specifically kevin  we really like containers and cloud run  those host containers without having to  worry about the service  if a server is acting up google fix is a  problem and we don't have to  also we like the billing model we pay  for container time even if the container  is handling multiple quests at the same  time  you mentioned building uh how much are  you actually paying for cloud run  our cloud one bill has been growing so  i'm keeping an eye on  last month we handled six million  requests with cloud run and we paid two  thousand claims kronos and that's about  320 us dollars  not too bad um how do you deploy your  containers to cloud run  when a polarquest is merged into the  main branch in our source control a  trigger ultimately builds and deploys  the container to our staging environment  and that happens about 50 times a week  and when we're done testing that  particular  release we mainly run a script to deploy  that container to our production  environment and that also happens about  50 times per week  are you building the containers on one  of your machines or up in the cloud  how are we using cloud builds in google  cloud platform all right gear our build  was  recently getting slow and taken 15  minutes and but it was easy for us to  change that machine type in one line or  in our cloud builder yammer  which reduced the build time to five  minutes only  oh nice so when you do 50 deployments  per week  that's a lot how do you keep these  deployments straight  we're big fans of immutable deployments  when cloudrun deploys a container  you can tweak settings and environment  variables instead you do a new  deployment  so immutable deployments prevent drifts  in our production environment  ah that makes sense kevin i'd love to  hear more about that  maybe you could tell us more details in  the next episode sounds good  and thank you everyone for watching if  you have questions for kevin or me  please add them in the comments also  this episode was a little different from  others in the serverless expedition  series please let us know if you found  it useful until next time  [Music]
 

